WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.077
Imagine three threads sharing an event source.
Thread A is the leader waiting for the next

00:00:05.077 --> 00:00:09.928
event while B and C are followers. When an event
arrives, A arranges for a follower to

00:00:09.928 --> 00:00:12.918
become the new leader, then handles the event
itself.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.075
B can now wait for another event while A
performs application work. Draw leadership as a

00:00:18.075 --> 00:00:23.207
role that moves between threads, not as a
permanently special machine. The handoff avoids

00:00:23.207 --> 00:00:27.936
having every waiting thread compete
unnecessarily for the same event notification.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.096
The design also keeps event waiting separate
from potentially slower handling. Its value

00:00:33.096 --> 00:00:38.365
depends on the event system and workload, so the
performance argument should identify which

00:00:38.365 --> 00:00:41.607
coordination or scheduling overhead is actually
reduced.

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:46.676
Now compare this thread pool with a replicated
database. The thread-pool leader waits on an

00:00:46.676 --> 00:00:51.745
event source and coordinates dispatch within a
process. A database leader may accept writes

00:00:51.745 --> 00:00:56.592
and coordinate durable state across machines.
Replacing a waiting thread does not solve

00:00:56.592 --> 00:01:01.549
replica agreement after a network partition, and
it does not imply that acknowledged data

00:01:01.549 --> 00:01:03.053
survives a machine failure.

00:01:03.107 --> 00:01:07.847
Keeping the two meanings separate prevents an
architecture explanation from borrowing

00:01:07.847 --> 00:01:12.922
guarantees from an unrelated pattern. Within the
thread pool, still examine what happens if

00:01:12.922 --> 00:01:18.052
a handler blocks, whether another thread can
continue receiving events, and how cancellation

00:01:18.052 --> 00:01:18.386
works.

00:01:18.607 --> 00:01:23.601
Correct handoff protects the shared event
source; it is not a substitute for timeouts or

00:01:23.601 --> 00:01:25.246
bounded work in the handlers.

00:01:25.246 --> 00:01:30.468
A partitioned log divides records among several
ordered sequences. Each partition can have a

00:01:30.468 --> 00:01:35.689
leader handling its operations and followers
copying its records. Different brokers can lead

00:01:35.689 --> 00:01:40.287
different partitions, so leadership is
distributed across the cluster rather than

00:01:40.287 --> 00:01:42.387
concentrated in one universal writer.

00:01:42.387 --> 00:01:47.633
Within a partition, record positions provide an
order and consumers track their progress.

00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:52.880
Across partitions, there is no automatic single
global order. For order events, using the

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.891
order identifier as the partitioning key can
keep one order's sequence together while

00:01:57.891 --> 00:02:00.072
unrelated orders proceed in parallel.

00:02:00.072 --> 00:02:04.939
Draw partition boundaries before drawing leader
and follower arrows. Otherwise a diagram

00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:09.806
with several brokers can misleadingly suggest
that every record is ordered against every

00:02:09.806 --> 00:02:11.686
other record in the entire system.

00:02:11.686 --> 00:02:16.357
Suppose a leader accepts a record and fails
before a follower receives it. Whether an

00:02:16.357 --> 00:02:21.082
acknowledged record can be lost depends on the
acknowledgement and election rules, not

00:02:21.082 --> 00:02:25.917
merely on the presence of a follower box in the
diagram. A lagging replica should not be

00:02:25.917 --> 00:02:30.807
treated as an equally current replacement
without considering the durability consequence.

00:02:30.807 --> 00:02:36.068
Downstream, a payment worker can read a safely
replicated record and still crash around an

00:02:36.068 --> 00:02:41.270
external charge. Log durability and effect
correctness are separate boundaries. Trace the

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:46.589
record from producer acknowledgement through
replication, consumption, and external result.

00:02:46.589 --> 00:02:51.850
The historical coordination service shown in
older diagrams is an implementation detail of

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:56.993
that version; preserve the reasoning about
leadership, progress, and failure rather than

00:02:56.993 --> 00:02:59.098
assuming the topology never changes.
